WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.000
Last time, each beat ended where two lines crossed: the ventricle's line and the

2
00:00:05.000 --> 00:00:08.680
arteries' line. But a loop also has a floor.

3
00:00:09.500 --> 00:00:12.060
When a heart fails, which line has moved?

4
00:00:15.517 --> 00:00:18.517
Under the loop runs a third line, a soft curve.

5
00:00:19.777 --> 00:00:24.577
It is the passive filling curve, the end-diastolic pressure-volume relation.

6
00:00:25.917 --> 00:00:29.677
Once relaxation is complete, the bottom of the loop lies on it.

7
00:00:30.697 --> 00:00:32.617
Its slope indexes chamber stiffness.

8
00:00:33.750 --> 00:00:37.210
Start with heart failure with reduced ejection fraction.

9
00:00:38.490 --> 00:00:42.470
Remodelling shifts the ventricle's line, the floor and the loop to the right.

10
00:00:43.690 --> 00:00:48.130
The ventricle's line is usually flatter, its contractility index lower.

11
00:00:49.410 --> 00:00:53.790
The result is a large chamber ejecting a small fraction of its volume.

12
00:00:54.833 --> 00:01:00.053
So ask of every sick loop, not only how much it ejects, but which line has moved.

13
00:01:01.313 --> 00:01:03.133
The ventricle's line tracks the squeeze.

14
00:01:04.473 --> 00:01:05.613
The floor tracks filling.

15
00:01:06.833 --> 00:01:08.633
The arteries' line tracks the load.

16
00:01:09.667 --> 00:01:12.607
Now heart failure with preserved ejection fraction.

17
00:01:14.007 --> 00:01:15.167
Here the floor moves.

18
00:01:16.547 --> 00:01:20.147
Catheter studies found the filling curve shifted up and to the left.

19
00:01:21.727 --> 00:01:24.467
Passive stiffness was higher, and relaxation was slower.

20
00:01:25.567 --> 00:01:29.427
Fill this stiffer chamber to the same volume as a normal one.

21
00:01:30.487 --> 00:01:34.187
What happens to the pressure at the bottom right corner, where filling ends?

22
00:01:40.200 --> 00:01:46.400
It runs higher. On a floor shifted up and left, the same volume sits at a higher

23
00:01:46.400 --> 00:01:52.540
pressure. A catheter study of 70 people with this condition found stiffer chambers

24
00:01:52.540 --> 00:01:59.320
at rest. When handgrip raised afterload, their end-diastolic pressures rose more

25
00:01:59.320 --> 00:02:00.640
than in the comparison group.

26
00:02:01.440 --> 00:02:04.200
The study did not show what causes the stiffness.

27
00:02:05.367 --> 00:02:09.307
In a small catheter study, the ventricle's line was steeper too.

28
00:02:10.567 --> 00:02:12.647
But that slope belongs to the whole chamber.

29
00:02:14.007 --> 00:02:16.487
Thick walls and passive stiffness can steepen it.

30
00:02:17.627 --> 00:02:22.307
In one community study, the slope was raised, yet contractility was depressed.

31
00:02:23.607 --> 00:02:25.827
A steeper line need not mean stronger muscle.

32
00:02:26.950 --> 00:02:30.770
In aortic stenosis, the narrowed valve adds to afterload.

33
00:02:32.110 --> 00:02:35.610
Ventricular pressure climbs, and the top of the loop domes upward.

34
00:02:36.930 --> 00:02:41.410
A thickened wall can keep ejection fraction near normal, until the ventricle starts

35
00:02:41.410 --> 00:02:42.410
to fail.

36
00:02:43.033 --> 00:02:48.933
Reduced ejection fraction, preserved ejection fraction, a narrowed valve: each moves

37
00:02:48.933 --> 00:02:54.033
different lines. Read the loop for where the trouble sits, in the squeeze, the

38
00:02:54.033 --> 00:02:55.093
filling or the load.
